        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The widespread occurrence of  statocytes with sedimenting statoliths in higher plants is compelling evidence that they are involved in some way with the process of detection of the g-force vector direction.  Whether the same process serves to detect the intensity of that force is not well established.  The transduction mechanism by which positional information is translated into meaningful biological changes is also not understood.  Determination of the smallest stimulus capable of eliciting a response, referred to as threshold, was an important experiment objective.  Other objectives were to determine the maximum response to laterally directed g-acceleration between zero and one g,  to determine the sensitivity of the plant's response parameter and to determine whether the plant's response is proportional to the intensity of the stimulation.<P>
APPROACH:<br>
The test organism was <i>Avena sativa</i>.  Nine seeds were planted in 5 groups of 4 cubes.  The seedlings were raised in darkness.  Prior to testing, the seedlings were in space under centrifugation at 1 g.   Tropistic stimulations were produced by laterally directed centripetal accelerations on variable speed centrifuges that were programmed to apply stimulus doses where both centrifuge speed and duration of stimulation were varied.  Stimulus doses were defined as <i>g</i> x <i>t</i> in which <i>g</i> and <i>t</i> were independent variables.  In different tests, centripetal accelerations were varied between 0.1 and 1.0 g and <i>t</i> was varied from 2  to 130 min.  Stimulus doses were varied from 1.8 to 25.2 min.  Temperatures were controlled and recorded continuously.  The nominal temperature was 22.5 degrees C.  Tropistic response was measured before and after stimulation with time lapse images using infrared light.   Gas samples were taken from 3 cubes and analyzed for oxygen, carbon dioxide and ethylene content.<P>
RESULTS:<br>
To determine threshold stimulation times, logarithmic curve fitting was used.  Characteristic qualitative features of the Avena coleoptile's tropistic response pattern in weightlessness were not greatly different from ground based studies.  For flight data, logarithmic plots extrapolated to positive threshold values in the range 0.9 to 7.8 min.  The shortest stimulation time to cause a response at 1.0 g was 2.0 min., and by extrapolation, the threshold was less than 1 min.  The least g-value used to stimulate coleoptiles in flight was 0.1 g.  In that experiment, a significant response of 19.6 plus or minus 2.2 degrees was observed, which demonstrated that if there is an absolute g-threshold for gravitropic response, it must be below  0.1 g.   Qualitatively, autotropic counterreactions after initial tropistic bending were evident from visual inspection of time series of response images.  As a general trend, autotropic responses were more prominent the greater the stimulus and the tropistic response.
        </div>

